=== 1. WHAT THE CENTRING SUBTRACTS ===

Claim to verify: y_i_mc = y_i - mean(y_i within focal variable).

  focal   n mean_raw mean_centred     max_dev
1 brand 491  4.96741 -4.48065e-11 4.48062e-11
2   rep 491  4.04684  1.89409e-10 1.89410e-10
3   pop 491  4.52546  4.72505e-10 4.72505e-10

Overall mean of y_i: 4.513238 
Largest deviation from the within-focal rule: 4.73e-10 

So the constant subtracted is different for each cue: it is not one
centring but three. The three cues are moved to a common zero, which is
exactly what makes the levels no longer comparable (see §4).


=== 2. WHERE THE THESIS USES IT ===

`y_i_mc` appears in the 2023 script only inside the four interaction.plot
calls that produce Figure 3.1 (lines 118-138 of thesis_analysis_2023.R).
Every regression uses the raw outcome: the nine lm() calls take y_rep_*,
y_pop_*, y_brand_*, and the repeated-measures ANOVA takes y_i. The
centred outcome therefore carries no estimate in the thesis; it carries a
figure, and one sentence of the Discussion that reads the figure.


=== 3. WOULD ANY ESTIMATE HAVE CHANGED? ===

Subtracting a constant per cue cannot change a slope within that cue, so
in principle only the main effect of `focal` moves. Two things can break
that in practice: an unbalanced design, and terms that mix cues.

