Where the two orderings disagree, the figure shows an ordering that the
data does not have on its own scale. The thesis reads one thing from the
figure -- the parallel between n=1 and n=3 -- and that reading is safe,
because it is about slopes. A reader comparing heights would be misled.

  written: fig_14_centring.png


=== 5. WHY THE CUE CENTRING IS HARMLESS, AND WHEN CENTRING IS NOT ===

The general rule: subtracting a constant per group from the outcome is
harmless if and only if the model already contains that group as a term,
because the constants are then absorbed by the group intercepts. The nine
regressions of the thesis are estimated one cue at a time -- the cue is
the sample -- and the pooled models of this review carry `focal` as a
factor. Either way a per-cue constant is absorbed, which is what §3
observed rather than assumed.

The rule also says where the same operation would have done damage. In a
repeated-measures design the tempting centring is by respondent, and 41%
of the variance in ITD is between respondents (ICC, script 04). Doing it
to the outcome alone, with no respondent term in the model, is not a
neutral rescaling: each respondent's mean contains their own treated
observation, so part of the effect is subtracted from itself.

Variance removed by each centring:
  by cue        var(y) = 2.7731 -> var(y_mc) = 2.6317  (5.1% removed)
  by respondent var(y) = 2.7731 -> var(y_sc) = 1.2011  (56.7% removed)

                          estimator focal estimate      se
               OLS, no subject term brand   0.9761 0.14289
     mixed model (random intercept) brand   1.0500 0.12239
     subject fixed effects (within) brand   1.0973 0.13179
 outcome centred by respondent only brand   0.7234 0.08955
               OLS, no subject term   pop   0.1164 0.14274
     mixed model (random intercept)   pop   0.2240 0.12226
     subject fixed effects (within)   pop   0.2942 0.13168
 outcome centred by respondent only   pop   0.1906 0.08945
               OLS, no subject term   rep   0.8002 0.14284
     mixed model (random intercept)   rep   0.8461 0.12236
     subject fixed effects (within)   rep   0.8794 0.13180
 outcome centred by respondent only   rep   0.5647 0.08951

The three legitimate estimators bracket each other: the mixed model sits
between pooled OLS and the within estimator, as it should. Centring the
outcome alone attenuates every effect instead, against the within
estimate: brand -34%, reputation -36%, popularity -35%.
That is the trap the thesis did not fall into -- because it centred on the
cue, which its models already control for, and not on the respondent.


=== 6. VERDICT ===

The mean-centred ITD is a defensible plotting device and a harmless one
for inference: no estimate in the thesis depends on it, and had every
model used it, only the between-cue main effect would have changed --
to zero, by construction.

Two qualifications stand:
  (a) the label. 'Mean-centered ITD' suggests one centring on the overall
      mean. It is three centrings, one per cue, so heights in Figure 3.1
      are within-cue deviations and not comparable across cues.
  (b) the dimension left untouched. The variance that needed handling in
      this design is between respondents, and no centring can do it: it
      needs a respondent term in the model, which is what the integer
      Error(lfdn) failed to provide and what the mixed model of script 04
      finally provides. Centring the outcome by respondent instead would
      have shrunk every effect by about a third (§5).
